[[:db/add "a3dfa19a-6690-5b37-a4d3-16184ec12210" :answer/id #uuid "a3dfa19a-6690-5b37-a4d3-16184ec12210"] [:db/add "a3dfa19a-6690-5b37-a4d3-16184ec12210" :answer/type :answer.type/math] [:db/add "a3dfa19a-6690-5b37-a4d3-16184ec12210" :answer/value "4{e}^{4x}-10{e}^{3x}"] [:db/ensure "a3dfa19a-6690-5b37-a4d3-16184ec12210" :answer/validate] [:db/add "9aad149d-b776-594a-8b0f-dde71ca18723" :answer-field/id #uuid "9aad149d-b776-594a-8b0f-dde71ca18723"] [:db/add "9aad149d-b776-594a-8b0f-dde71ca18723" :answer-field/key "field-1"] [:db/add "9aad149d-b776-594a-8b0f-dde71ca18723" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank] [:db/add "9aad149d-b776-594a-8b0f-dde71ca18723" :answer-field/choices "a3dfa19a-6690-5b37-a4d3-16184ec12210"] [:db/add "9aad149d-b776-594a-8b0f-dde71ca18723" :answer-field/correct "a3dfa19a-6690-5b37-a4d3-16184ec12210"] [:db/ensure "9aad149d-b776-594a-8b0f-dde71ca18723" :answer-field/validate] [:db/add "7751656d-de90-5f27-a82a-a3ddc66b626a" :question/id #uuid "7751656d-de90-5f27-a82a-a3ddc66b626a"] [:db/add "7751656d-de90-5f27-a82a-a3ddc66b626a" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y=10{e}^{3x},\\,y(0)=-6\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y=10{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/add "7751656d-de90-5f27-a82a-a3ddc66b626a" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $10{e}^{3x},$ and ${e}^{3x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=3\\alpha {e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3\\alpha {e}^{3x})-4(\\alpha {e}^{3x}) & =10{e}^{3x} \\\\ (3-4)\\alpha {e}^{3x} & =10{e}^{3x} \\\\ -\\alpha {e}^{3x} & =10{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-10{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4x}-10{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=-6$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-10 & =-6 \\\\ A & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=4{e}^{4x}-10{e}^{3x}.\n$$"] [:db/add "7751656d-de90-5f27-a82a-a3ddc66b626a" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] [:db/add "7751656d-de90-5f27-a82a-a3ddc66b626a" :question/answer-fields "9aad149d-b776-594a-8b0f-dde71ca18723"] [:db/ensure "7751656d-de90-5f27-a82a-a3ddc66b626a" :question/validate] [:db/add 17592186229165 :knowledge-point/questions "7751656d-de90-5f27-a82a-a3ddc66b626a"] [:db/add "afc1be2d-b249-5b7c-95cd-5254e3e2303d" :answer/id #uuid "afc1be2d-b249-5b7c-95cd-5254e3e2303d"] [:db/add "afc1be2d-b249-5b7c-95cd-5254e3e2303d" :answer/type :answer.type/math] [:db/add "afc1be2d-b249-5b7c-95cd-5254e3e2303d" :answer/value "\\frac{13}{3}{e}^{-5x}+\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{4x}"] [:db/ensure "afc1be2d-b249-5b7c-95cd-5254e3e2303d" :answer/validate] [:db/add "7d34ad3d-5eb8-561f-b712-48071750744d" :answer-field/id #uuid "7d34ad3d-5eb8-561f-b712-48071750744d"] [:db/add "7d34ad3d-5eb8-561f-b712-48071750744d" :answer-field/key "field-1"] [:db/add "7d34ad3d-5eb8-561f-b712-48071750744d" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank] [:db/add "7d34ad3d-5eb8-561f-b712-48071750744d" :answer-field/choices "afc1be2d-b249-5b7c-95cd-5254e3e2303d"] [:db/add "7d34ad3d-5eb8-561f-b712-48071750744d" :answer-field/correct "afc1be2d-b249-5b7c-95cd-5254e3e2303d"] [:db/ensure "7d34ad3d-5eb8-561f-b712-48071750744d" :answer-field/validate] [:db/add "6c3a86d8-ae5c-514d-924c-56a2a4c4c7d1" :question/id #uuid "6c3a86d8-ae5c-514d-924c-56a2a4c4c7d1"] [:db/add "6c3a86d8-ae5c-514d-924c-56a2a4c4c7d1" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+5y=6{e}^{4x},\\,y(0)=5\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+5y=6{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{-5x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/add "6c3a86d8-ae5c-514d-924c-56a2a4c4c7d1" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+5{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-5x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $6{e}^{4x},$ and ${e}^{4x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=4\\alpha {e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4\\alpha {e}^{4x})+5(\\alpha {e}^{4x}) & =6{e}^{4x} \\\\ (4+5)\\alpha {e}^{4x} & =6{e}^{4x} \\\\ 9\\alpha {e}^{4x} & =6{e}^{4x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-5x}+\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=5$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+\\frac{2}{3} & =5 \\\\ A & =\\frac{13}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{13}{3}{e}^{-5x}+\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{4x}.\n$$"] [:db/add "6c3a86d8-ae5c-514d-924c-56a2a4c4c7d1" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] [:db/add "6c3a86d8-ae5c-514d-924c-56a2a4c4c7d1" :question/answer-fields "7d34ad3d-5eb8-561f-b712-48071750744d"] [:db/ensure "6c3a86d8-ae5c-514d-924c-56a2a4c4c7d1" :question/validate] [:db/add 17592186229165 :knowledge-point/questions "6c3a86d8-ae5c-514d-924c-56a2a4c4c7d1"] [:db/add "88b835c1-50c4-512d-bdc2-b620f146d449" :answer/id #uuid "88b835c1-50c4-512d-bdc2-b620f146d449"] [:db/add "88b835c1-50c4-512d-bdc2-b620f146d449" :answer/type :answer.type/math] [:db/add "88b835c1-50c4-512d-bdc2-b620f146d449" :answer/value "-6{e}^{6x}-{e}^{-6x}"] [:db/ensure "88b835c1-50c4-512d-bdc2-b620f146d449" :answer/validate] [:db/add "99caa933-48b2-5392-86f6-d8881556de6b" :answer-field/id #uuid "99caa933-48b2-5392-86f6-d8881556de6b"] [:db/add "99caa933-48b2-5392-86f6-d8881556de6b" :answer-field/key "field-1"] [:db/add "99caa933-48b2-5392-86f6-d8881556de6b" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank] [:db/add "99caa933-48b2-5392-86f6-d8881556de6b" :answer-field/choices "88b835c1-50c4-512d-bdc2-b620f146d449"] [:db/add "99caa933-48b2-5392-86f6-d8881556de6b" :answer-field/correct "88b835c1-50c4-512d-bdc2-b620f146d449"] [:db/ensure "99caa933-48b2-5392-86f6-d8881556de6b" :answer-field/validate] [:db/add "6507ad75-48f6-531f-8654-44d77d1edbe5" :question/id #uuid "6507ad75-48f6-531f-8654-44d77d1edbe5"] [:db/add "6507ad75-48f6-531f-8654-44d77d1edbe5" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-6y=12{e}^{-6x},\\,y(0)=-7\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-6y=12{e}^{-6x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{6x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/add "6507ad75-48f6-531f-8654-44d77d1edbe5" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-6{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{6x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $12{e}^{-6x},$ and ${e}^{-6x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{-6x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=-6\\alpha {e}^{-6x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-6\\alpha {e}^{-6x})-6(\\alpha {e}^{-6x}) & =12{e}^{-6x} \\\\ (-6-6)\\alpha {e}^{-6x} & =12{e}^{-6x} \\\\ -12\\alpha {e}^{-6x} & =12{e}^{-6x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-{e}^{-6x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{6x}-{e}^{-6x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=-7$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-1 & =-7 \\\\ A & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-6{e}^{6x}-{e}^{-6x}.\n$$"] [:db/add "6507ad75-48f6-531f-8654-44d77d1edbe5" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] [:db/add "6507ad75-48f6-531f-8654-44d77d1edbe5" :question/answer-fields "99caa933-48b2-5392-86f6-d8881556de6b"] [:db/ensure "6507ad75-48f6-531f-8654-44d77d1edbe5" :question/validate] [:db/add 17592186229165 :knowledge-point/questions "6507ad75-48f6-531f-8654-44d77d1edbe5"] [:db/add "6a4eb57b-e6ba-5ef5-821a-9e8223d3da39" :answer/id #uuid "6a4eb57b-e6ba-5ef5-821a-9e8223d3da39"] [:db/add "6a4eb57b-e6ba-5ef5-821a-9e8223d3da39" :answer/type :answer.type/math] [:db/add "6a4eb57b-e6ba-5ef5-821a-9e8223d3da39" :answer/value "-\\frac{57}{7}{e}^{-2x}+\\frac{1}{7}{e}^{5x}"] [:db/ensure "6a4eb57b-e6ba-5ef5-821a-9e8223d3da39" :answer/validate] [:db/add "7bb13117-ae20-539e-b607-119478d2353a" :answer-field/id #uuid "7bb13117-ae20-539e-b607-119478d2353a"] [:db/add "7bb13117-ae20-539e-b607-119478d2353a" :answer-field/key "field-1"] [:db/add "7bb13117-ae20-539e-b607-119478d2353a" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank] [:db/add "7bb13117-ae20-539e-b607-119478d2353a" :answer-field/choices "6a4eb57b-e6ba-5ef5-821a-9e8223d3da39"] [:db/add "7bb13117-ae20-539e-b607-119478d2353a" :answer-field/correct "6a4eb57b-e6ba-5ef5-821a-9e8223d3da39"] [:db/ensure "7bb13117-ae20-539e-b607-119478d2353a" :answer-field/validate] [:db/add "d3a814e3-11d3-5c6d-aded-548b37dd73b4" :question/id #uuid "d3a814e3-11d3-5c6d-aded-548b37dd73b4"] [:db/add "d3a814e3-11d3-5c6d-aded-548b37dd73b4" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y={e}^{5x},\\,y(0)=-8\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y={e}^{5x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/add "d3a814e3-11d3-5c6d-aded-548b37dd73b4" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is ${e}^{5x},$ and ${e}^{5x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=5\\alpha {e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5\\alpha {e}^{5x})+2(\\alpha {e}^{5x}) & ={e}^{5x} \\\\ (5+2)\\alpha {e}^{5x} & ={e}^{5x} \\\\ 7\\alpha {e}^{5x} & ={e}^{5x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{1}{7}{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+\\frac{1}{7}{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=-8$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+\\frac{1}{7} & =-8 \\\\ A & =-\\frac{57}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{57}{7}{e}^{-2x}+\\frac{1}{7}{e}^{5x}.\n$$"] [:db/add "d3a814e3-11d3-5c6d-aded-548b37dd73b4" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] [:db/add "d3a814e3-11d3-5c6d-aded-548b37dd73b4" :question/answer-fields "7bb13117-ae20-539e-b607-119478d2353a"] [:db/ensure "d3a814e3-11d3-5c6d-aded-548b37dd73b4" :question/validate] [:db/add 17592186229165 :knowledge-point/questions "d3a814e3-11d3-5c6d-aded-548b37dd73b4"] [:db/add "50920f78-64a7-524d-a082-3e3c7fa50c3c" :answer/id #uuid "50920f78-64a7-524d-a082-3e3c7fa50c3c"] [:db/add "50920f78-64a7-524d-a082-3e3c7fa50c3c" :answer/type :answer.type/math] [:db/add "50920f78-64a7-524d-a082-3e3c7fa50c3c" :answer/value "-6{e}^{-4x}+5{e}^{-3x}"] [:db/ensure "50920f78-64a7-524d-a082-3e3c7fa50c3c" :answer/validate] [:db/add "ec8c8fcd-18e1-59fa-bff5-93cd9b820b87" :answer-field/id #uuid "ec8c8fcd-18e1-59fa-bff5-93cd9b820b87"] [:db/add "ec8c8fcd-18e1-59fa-bff5-93cd9b820b87" :answer-field/key "field-1"] [:db/add "ec8c8fcd-18e1-59fa-bff5-93cd9b820b87" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank] [:db/add "ec8c8fcd-18e1-59fa-bff5-93cd9b820b87" :answer-field/choices "50920f78-64a7-524d-a082-3e3c7fa50c3c"] [:db/add "ec8c8fcd-18e1-59fa-bff5-93cd9b820b87" :answer-field/correct "50920f78-64a7-524d-a082-3e3c7fa50c3c"] [:db/ensure "ec8c8fcd-18e1-59fa-bff5-93cd9b820b87" :answer-field/validate] [:db/add "a79a19f5-81e1-5c0f-b705-f719ba52e841" :question/id #uuid "a79a19f5-81e1-5c0f-b705-f719ba52e841"] [:db/add "a79a19f5-81e1-5c0f-b705-f719ba52e841" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4y=5{e}^{-3x},\\,y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4y=5{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{-4x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/add "a79a19f5-81e1-5c0f-b705-f719ba52e841" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $5{e}^{-3x},$ and ${e}^{-3x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{-3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=-3\\alpha {e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3\\alpha {e}^{-3x})+4(\\alpha {e}^{-3x}) & =5{e}^{-3x} \\\\ (-3+4)\\alpha {e}^{-3x} & =5{e}^{-3x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-3x} & =5{e}^{-3x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=5{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-4x}+5{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=-1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+5 & =-1 \\\\ A & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-6{e}^{-4x}+5{e}^{-3x}.\n$$"] [:db/add "a79a19f5-81e1-5c0f-b705-f719ba52e841" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] [:db/add "a79a19f5-81e1-5c0f-b705-f719ba52e841" :question/answer-fields "ec8c8fcd-18e1-59fa-bff5-93cd9b820b87"] [:db/ensure "a79a19f5-81e1-5c0f-b705-f719ba52e841" :question/validate] [:db/add 17592186229165 :knowledge-point/questions "a79a19f5-81e1-5c0f-b705-f719ba52e841"] [:db/add "generator" :question-generator/id #uuid "0f10bfff-83e3-5983-9727-01bdfda99630"] [:db/add "generator" :question-generator/script "generators/a9/a96387a8